# UI Skeleton Parity

When implementing loading states in UI components:

- Keep all static content visible in loading state (titles, labels, helper text, section headings, fixed button text).
- Skeleton only dynamic regions where fetched/runtime data appears (table cell values, metric values, avatars/images, async chips/tags).
- Match final layout 1:1 in loading state: spacing, alignment, container widths, row heights, and responsive behavior.
- Match typography parity for static text: same font size, weight, line-height, and color classes as the loaded view.
- Keep table headers/toolbars visible; skeletonize rows/cells, not the entire table shell.

## Component Structure Rules

- Preferred: split into dedicated loading UI components (e.g. `FeatureTableSkeleton`) when layout is substantial.
- If current pattern is inline conditional rendering (`isLoading ? skeleton : data`), keep it and make parity accurate there.
- If only a small skeleton fragment exists, extract a full-page/component skeleton variant when that improves 1:1 visual parity.

## Quick Check Before Merge

- Compare loading and loaded views side-by-side for identical structure.
- Confirm no static copy disappears during loading.
- Confirm dynamic placeholders occupy the exact final value slots.
